whose face turns pale when he is seen, who turns away even from his friends, / and appears merely indifferent toward the women he loves;
who, even though knowing it is an auspicious time, does not put on adornments— / such a woman is disaffected. Now hear from me as I speak of one who is in love.
Merely upon seeing him she becomes delighted, and looks at him with her face turned away; / when she is looked at, she casts her restless gaze elsewhere;
yet she turns [her gaze] away, but cannot do so completely; / likewise, O descendant of Bhṛgu, she exposes limbs that should be well hidden;
and with effort she conceals an unblemished limb; / on seeing him, she hugs and kisses a child;
when addressed, her speech falters; / when touched, with horripilated limbs, she leans upon her female friends or [slumps] in weariness;
and likewise, O Rāma, she asks for articles that are easily obtained; / then, receiving even a little, she attains the highest joy;

at the mere mention of his name she rejoices and holds it dear; / she also sends him fruits marked with her fingernails;
and what is sent by him she places with reverence upon her heart; / and with her embraces she seems to smear his limbs with nectar;
when he is asleep she sleeps, but goes to sleep after him and wakes before him; / she touches his thighs repeatedly, and kisses him while he sleeps.
Having thus recognised a woman who is in love, a self-possessed man may love her. / Sensual desire, dining, and friendship—dolls should be understood
when one wishes to win over a young girl, along with toys and the like; / by offering perfumes and garlands one should bring a youthful woman under control;
and by giving clothes and ornaments, a woman whose youth has passed. / A young girl is fond of play and good company, and likewise one whose youth has passed;
a young woman is known to be fond of pleasure, or fond of both. / Self-importance should never be displayed before women.
Deep resentment arises toward a man who displays self-importance. / One should neither constantly grant them sight of oneself, nor remain entirely out of sight.
